From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:26:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(graphics): cap the memory the Progressive decoder keeps
 at 1 GiB

The RemoteFX Progressive decoder keeps the state a server asks for: a tile
grid sized for each surface it decodes into; a 36 KiB TileState for every
tile it fills; and a 24 KiB sub-band reference for every tile position of
a surface. Nothing bounded any of it, and only DeleteSurface or a reset
freed it. A server could fill tiles at about 60 KiB per tile block of a
few dozen bytes, or create many large surfaces, until the WebAssembly
instance every session shares ran out of memory.

The decoder now counts what it holds (tile grids, tiles and references)
and refuses a grid, tile or reference that would take it past
MAX_PROGRESSIVE_BYTES, 1 GiB, with ProgressiveDecodeError::MemoryLimitExceeded,
which drops the update the way a malformed tile does. Deleting a surface,
resetting, and resizing a surface give the memory back; deleting a codec
context releases nothing, since its surface's tiles stay (as upstream
keeps them since #2117). 1 GiB holds every tile of the 256 MiB of 32 bpp
surfaces the EGFX compositor displays at most (64 Mi pixels, two 8K
monitors), at about 60 KiB a tile.

A surface's tile grid is charged the size of its map entry and of its
slots, and SURFACE_OVERHEAD_BYTES, 512, for what holding them takes
besides: B-tree nodes about half full, for the surface and, while an
RDPGFX frame is open, its frame_tiles entry, and the grid's smallest
allocation.

For the count to match what is allocated, references are now boxed: a
B-tree node holds eleven values, and with 24 KiB values, the empty slots
of half-full nodes took up to as much memory again (a 4096-tile surface
held 326 MB, now 252 MB). A TILE_UPGRADE for a tile without a first pass
no longer allocates an empty tile.
